course: everyday
lesson: 35
title: Home alone, the door and the phone
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When a child is old enough to stay home alone for a short time, the family agrees on clear rules first: who may come in, what you may use, and how to reach a parent.
explain: The front door stays locked. You do not open it to anyone you were not told to expect, even if they seem friendly, say they are delivering something or say they know your parents.
explain: You never tell a caller or a stranger at the door that you are alone. A safe answer is "My mum can't come to the phone right now. Can I take a message?"
explain: For example, a man knocks and says he has come to read the gas meter. You do not open the door; you can say through the closed door that he must come back later, and then phone your parent. A real worker will understand.
explain: If someone tries to get in, or you smell gas or see smoke, you do not wait: you call the emergency number, and for gas or smoke you leave the house first.
explain: A boy home alone in Ohio opened the door to a man who said he was from the gas company; it was a thief who walked in and took things while the boy was frightened.
explain: A girl in Sydney posted online that her parents were out all evening; strangers saw it, and her family had to change the locks after someone tried their door.
fact: Before staying home alone, a child should agree on rules with their parents.
fact: The door should stay locked while you are home alone.
fact: You should not open the door to anyone you were not told to expect.
fact: You should never tell a stranger, on the phone, at the door or online, that you are home alone.
fact: You should know your parents' phone numbers and your own address by heart.
fact: You should not post online where you are or that you are home alone.
fact: If someone tries to break in, you call the emergency number at once.
fact: If you smell gas, you leave the house without switching anything on or off, because a tiny spark from a switch could set the gas alight, and call for help from outside.
fact: The gas used in homes is given a smell like rotten eggs, so that a leak is easy to notice.
fact: Your house key should be kept out of sight and should not have your name or address on it.
fact: If something feels wrong, it is always right to call your parent or a trusted neighbour.
fact: Telling strangers that you are home alone, at the door, on the phone or online, can put you in danger.
fact: A real worker or delivery person will come back later or leave a note.
exercise: A woman knocks while you are home alone and says she is your mum's friend. What should you do? | Keep the door shut and phone your mum | Open the door because she knows your mum | Let her in but keep watching her | Ask her to climb in through the window
exercise: The phone rings and a stranger asks, "Are your parents home?" What is a safe reply? | "They can't come to the phone. Can I take a message?" | "No, I'm all by myself until six." | "They are out, but the door is open." | "I'm alone right now, but you can call back later on."
exercise: A delivery driver says you must sign for a parcel. You are home alone. What should you do? | Ask through the door for it to be left or brought back later | Open the door wide and sign at once | Let the driver carry it into the kitchen | Go outside and help load the van
exercise: A girl in Austin is home alone and smells gas in the kitchen. What should she do? | Leave the house and call for help from outside | Switch on the light to find where the leak is coming from | Light a candle to see better | Open the oven to check it
exercise: Why should you not switch lights on or off when you smell gas? | A tiny spark from the switch could ignite the gas | Lights make gas smell stronger | Switches stop working when gas is near | Gas makes light bulbs explode all by themselves, even when off
exercise: Someone is trying to open your locked back door while you are alone. What should you do? | Call the emergency number straight away | Open the door to see who it is | Hide quietly and wait without calling anyone at all | Shout your name and address at them
exercise: You want to post a photo saying "Home alone all day!" Why is that unwise? | Strangers could learn that you are alone | It uses up too much of the phone's battery | Only adults are allowed to post photos | Your friends will be jealous of you
exercise: Why should your house key not have your address on its key ring? | Whoever finds a lost key would know which house it opens | Keys with labels on them do not turn properly in locks | The label makes the key too heavy | It looks untidy to other people
exercise: A man in uniform says he is from the water company and needs to come in. Your parents said nobody would visit. What should you do? | Keep the door locked and call your parent | Let him in because he is wearing a uniform | Open the door just a little to check | Ask him to wait inside while you call
exercise: Which two things should every child know by heart before staying home alone? | Their address and a parent's phone number | Their teacher's name and their class number | Their friends' birthdays and phone numbers | The route of the school bus and its driver
exercise: You hear a strange noise in the house and feel scared. What is a good first step? | Phone your parent or a trusted neighbour | Go and search the whole house by yourself | Turn up the TV so you cannot hear it | Open the front door and wait outside it
exercise: A boy in Toronto is alone and an online game player asks where he lives. What should he do? | Not answer and tell a parent | Give his street but not his house number | Tell him because they play the same game | Send a photo of his house instead
exercise: Your friend's older brother knocks and asks to come in and wait for your sister. Nobody told you he would come. What should you do? | Keep the door shut and phone a parent to ask | Let him in because you know who he is | Let him in only if he promises to be quick | Leave the door unlocked for him and go upstairs
test: A girl in Glasgow is home alone when the doorbell rings. A smiling woman says she is collecting money for charity. What should the girl do? | Not open the door and tell her parents later | Open the door and give her some coins | Invite her in while she looks for money | Tell her she is alone, so the woman will understand and leave
test: What would most likely happen if a child told a phone caller that they were home alone? | A person with bad plans would know the house has no adult | The caller would hang up and forget | The parents would come home sooner | Nothing, because callers cannot see the house
test: Which statement is true? | A real worker will come back later if no adult is home | Anyone in a uniform must be let in | Delivery drivers can come in to leave parcels in the hall | It is rude to keep a door locked when people knock
test: Jack is home alone and smells something like rotten eggs near the stove. What should he do? | Go outside and phone for help from there | Turn the stove knobs on and off to test them | Turn on the kitchen fan to blow it away | Call his friend from inside to ask what it is
test: A boy in Cork loses his house key on the way home. Why is it good that the key had no label? | A finder could not tell which home it opens | A lost key with no label turns up sooner | Labels make keys rust and break | The key will work in any house now, so it doesn't matter
test: Someone knocks again and again and tries the door handle while Ella is home alone. What should Ella do? | Phone 999 or the local emergency number at once | Open the door and ask them to leave | Hide and hope they go away, calling nobody | Unlock the back door and wait outside
test: Your parent texts that a neighbour, Mrs Lee, will drop off dinner. Mrs Lee knocks. What can you do? | Check it is Mrs Lee and then take the dinner | Refuse to answer anyone at all, even Mrs Lee | Ask Mrs Lee to stay the whole evening | Tell Mrs Lee to leave it at the shop
test: Which is the safest thing to say to a stranger asking for your dad on the phone? | "He's busy right now. Can I take a message?" | "He's at work until late tonight." | "He went away, I'm on my own." | "He's out; come round to the house and wait for him."
test: Why do families agree on rules before a child stays home alone? | So the child knows what to do before anything happens | So the child can do anything they like | So the parents can stay out longer | So the neighbours know the house is empty and keep an eye on it
test: home alone, doorbell, don't know them | Don't open the door | Open it and ask who it is | Open it if they smile | Let them in to wait
test: someone rang, asked is ur mum home, she's not | Don't say you're alone, say she can't come to the phone | Say yes, she's out | Give them your address | Tell them when she's back
test: post on insta that i'm home alone, bored | No, never say online you're home alone | Yes, if it's just friends | Yes, with a photo | Yes, if you add your street so friends can visit
test: someone trying to get in the back door!! | Call the emergency number at once | Go and open it | Hide and wait | Shout through the door that you're on your own
test: someone at the door says he's from the water company, im home alone | Do not open it, tell him to come back later | Open it, he has a uniform | Open it a little on the chain to see his badge | Let him in if he is polite
test: phone rings, guy asks if mum's home (she's not) | Say she cannot come to the phone and take a message | Say she is out until late | Tell him you are alone | Give him her work address so he can go and find her there
test: gonna post a pic, home alone all weekend!! | Do not post it, strangers could see you are alone | Post it, only friends see it anyway, so it's safe | Post it with no location | Post it but use a filter
test: heard a kid answered the door home alone and the guy said he was from the gas company but he was a robber | Real lesson: do not open the door to anyone you were not told to expect | Real lesson: gas workers are always robbers | It proves children can never be home alone | It is gossip, so opening the door is fine
test: my mate says when someone rings and asks for ur mum, just say she's out and ur alone, it's polite | Not safe; never tell a caller you are home alone, say she cannot come to the phone | He is right, honesty means saying you are alone | He is right, if the caller sounds nice | He is right, but give your address too
test: they're saying Jack's left home alone every night cos his mum doesn't care, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is unkind and you do not know their story | Pass it on, so a teacher hears | Pass it on, since everyone already knows | Pass it on, and add that he has no food
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the proper treatment of the front door whilst a child is home alone? | The door should remain locked and not be opened to unexpected callers | The door should be left open for fresh air | The door should be opened to anyone in uniform | The door should be opened to anyone who knocks twice
exam: Identify the information which a child must never disclose to a stranger, by telephone, at the door or online. | That the child is home alone | The name of the child's school sports team | The weather in the child's town | The child's favourite colour
exam: It being given that a person attempts to break into the dwelling, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To call the emergency number at once | To open the door and demand that the person leave | To post a message online about the matter | To hide and remain silent without telling anyone
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning a house key. | It should be kept out of sight and bear neither the child's name nor address | It should be labelled with the child's full address | It should be left beneath the mat for convenience | It should be worn visibly around the neck
